<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the BUNDLED ENGINE and every Mach-O underneath it: the
  relocatable CPython in Contents/Resources/payload/runtime, its extension
  modules in payload/pylib, and the payload/bin/opendj-engine launcher.

  ONE KEY, DELIBERATELY. com.apple.security.inherit means "take the parent's
  sandbox exactly", and macOS enforces that a binary carrying it may carry no
  other entitlement. Adding network.server here (the tempting mistake, since
  the engine is the process that actually binds the port) makes the signature
  invalid rather than more permissive: the child gets the parent's network
  entitlement through inheritance already.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, rather than signing the whole bundle in one
  pass: codesign's "deep" flag walks into Resources and re-signs the payload
  with the PARENT's entitlements, which would hand a full sandbox profile to a
  Python interpreter. Apple documents that flag as unsuitable for signing an
  app for distribution, and this is the concrete reason here. Sign inside-out
  instead, and never pass it. scripts/ship_appstore.sh does exactly that.

  (Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the
  flag is named in prose above rather than written out. A literal one here
  makes this file unparseable, which the signing step reports only as a
  confusing codesign failure.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.inherit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
